  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1251 AM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nUnseasonable warm 850mb temperatures (30 to 35C) remain in \nplace earlier this evening along and south of a surface trough \nof low pressure extending from northeast Kansas into eastern \nColorado. Along/near this boundary, much like the past few \nevenings, a few isolated high based storms developed across\neastern Colorado and northwest Kansas. While these storm were \nsub severe...gusty winds in excess of 50 mph were possible given\nthe large low level lapse rates and forecast downdraft cape. \nThis scenario (<15% chance for late day storms) will again be \npossible Tuesday night near/in west central Kansas as the \nsurface trough remains nearly stationary and a subtle upper \nlevel wave rotates around an upper level ridge. Although severe \nweather is not expected, any storm that does develop will be \ncapable of producing strong gusty winds. \n\nThe surface trough of low pressure will not be the only feature\nnot to move much over the next few days...anomalously warm \n850mb temperatures will also remain in place across southwest \nKansas through mid week as the upper ridge near the four corners\nregion moves east into the southern/central plains. As this \nupper ridge slides east these 850mb temperatures may even warm \na degree or two between 00z Tuesday and 00z Thursday. As a \nresult high temperatures across southwest Kansas will stay near \nthe same as what occurred on Monday or maybe even a few degrees \nwarmer. NBM guidance continues to have a cool bias on high \ntemperatures with the 75th to 90th percentile doing a much \nbetter job with verification. Also this trend towards warmer \nhighs is further supported by the ongoing forecast of 850mb \ntemperatures at 00z each day ranging from 32 to 35C, 700mb \ntemperatures of 14 to 16C, and continue deep mixing potential. \nRegarding dewpoints across southwest Kansas, the south \nsouthwesterly low level flow and afternoon mixing will likely \nkeep afternoon dewpoints in the 50s with the lowest dewpoints \nexpected across far west Kansas. Based on the expected highs and\nafternoon dewpoints it appears that the heat index values will \nnot be much higher on Tuesday and Wednesday compared to what we \nhad today (afternoon heat index ranging from of 102 to near \n108). This is the third straight day of heat index values at or\nabove 102 across all of southwest Kansas. A heat advisory has \nalready been issued for locations east of highway 283 but given \nthis will be the third day of heat index values at or above 102 \nand that the past overnight lows were/will be the mid 70s, which\nlimits the recovery time from afternoon heat, have decided \nexpand the heat advisory westward to the Colorado border. \n\nAlong with the heat on Tuesday there will also be an increase \nin the fire weather risk. While current forecast dewpoints may \nkeep afternoon heat index values just below 105 in some \nlocations...they will produce afternoon humidity values as low \nas 17 to 20 percent, mainly near the Oklahoma border and west of\nhighway 83. These low humidity values combined with gusty south\nwinds will create an environment that will make it easier for \nfires to escape control and be more difficult to contain. \nOutdoor burning is discouraged, especially west of highway 83 \nand near the Oklahoma border.\n\nSome relief from the extended heat is anticipated by the start \nof the weekend. As the upper level ridge shift eastward and a \nupper trough moves/develop over the western United States, a \ncold front will sag south into western Kansas. This front will \nbring more seasonal temperatures back to north central and \nsouthwest Kansas by the beginning of the weekend. In addition to\nthese more seasonal temperatures the chance of rain will be on \nthe increase as several upper waves embedded in the developing \nsouthwest flow moves from the southwest United States into the \nPlains.",
